{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/dao.star.md'","aggregated_output":"# dao.star: Hypermedia Web Applications in datom.world\n\nStatus: design; Mode 1 ratified by owner ruling (2026-10-24). Mode 1 is\nspecified and buildable on [`dao.stream.http.md`](./dao.stream.http.md) and\n[`dao.lease.md`](./dao.lease.md).\nMode 2 is proposed and waits on fenced custody, version 1 of\n[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md)\n(owner ruling 2026-10-07). The published framing is\n[`daostar-vs-datastar-vs-electric-clojure.blog`](../../public/chp/blog/daostar-vs-datastar-vs-electric-clojure.blog).\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\n`dao.star` is not a library or an adapter; it is the architectural pattern for building web applications natively in `datom.world`. It emerges from the intersection of two aligned philosophies:\n1. **datom.world**: Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data flowing over explicit, append-only streams (Axioms 1 and 2). It avoids compiler magic, instead exposing the network explicitly via data.\n2. **Datastar**: The browser should be a dumb, stateless interpreter of hypermedia (HTML) delivered over Server-Sent Events (SSE).\n\nDatastar interprets the SSE projection of an egress `dao.stream`. That is enough to eliminate frontend state management, reactive DAG compilers (like Electric Clojure), and proprietary RPC adapters: standard HTTP in, standard SSE out, and every piece in between is an ordinary stream or an ordinary interpreter.\n\n`dao.star` provides two operational modes under one programming model: **The Thin Client** (Arc-style sequential UI) and **The Fat Client** (mobile continuations).\n\n**Roles, not nodes.** \"Server\" below means the peer whose composition holds the HTTP boundary; \"browser\" means a peer that can only dial. Neither is a kind of node, and no protocol privilege attaches to either (`datom.world.md`, Design Principles). The HTTP boundary's two media are ordinary streams, so the agent answering requests may run on a peer other than the one that listens, over `dao.stream.remote.md` reflections.\n\n---\n\n## Mode 1: The Thin Client (Arc-Style Sequential UI)\n\nIn the thin client model the browser holds zero business logic and zero shared mutable state. Datastar acts as a dumb terminal rendering HTML patches, while `yin.vm` executes linear, sequential code on the server.\n\n### Flow\n\n1. **Events In**: A user clicks a button (`data-on-click=\"@post('/k/<token>')\"`). Datastar fires a standard HTTP request. The generic HTTP boundary (`dao.stream.http.md`) mints an exchange id, deposits the request as an unjudged `:http/request` event into the ingress `dao.stream` the composition wired, and holds the socket open. It reads nothing inside the request.\n2. **Routing**: A router step in the `dao.star` composition reads the ingress with its own cursor, classifies each request by its path (*Sessions and request kinds*), and forwards it to the medium of the session its token names, together with every later terminal event of that exchange. The boundary does not route.\n3. **Resuming**: The session's driver, on its next poll, observes the input at its own cursor and steps the parked continuation. Nothing wakes anything; a stream invokes nothing.\n4. **Semantics and State**: The `yin.vm` agent evaluates business logic linearly. The UI is a derivation of the continuation's state, never stored: the agent projects it into Datastar's SSE wire format (`event: datastar-patch-elements\\ndata: <div>...</div>`) through one pure projection function the composition supplies.\n5. **Effects Out**: The agent appends to the egress `dao.stream`, tagged with the exchange id: an `:http/head` effect (status, `Content-Type: text/event-stream`), one `:http/chunk` per SSE event with the fully constructed string, and `:http/end` when the response is complete. A subscription exchange simply never ends.\n6. **Dumb Socket Routing**: The boundary's `egress-step` observes the egress stream, matches the exchange id to the open socket, and flushes the unmodified payload. Datastar morphs the DOM. The agent parks again, waiting for the next input, for as long as it takes.\n\n### Sessions and request kinds\n\nA **lineage** is one session's chain of occurrences: a root and every successor a consumed input produced. **The token is the occurrence id**, minted unguessable by the driver; the lineage's identity is its root's id. Every projected UI carries the id of the occurrence that rendered it. The lineage's **current** occurrence is the one with no successor.\n\nThe driver appends one fact per transition to the **session-facts** medium, and appends it with `:dao.stream/ok` before any UI carrying the new id is projected:\n\n```clojure\n{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/occurrence S}                            ; root\n{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/occurrence O2 :dao.star/predecessor O1}  ; consumption\n{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/ended :halted}                           ; the program finished\n```\n\nThe router materializes occurrence → lineage → current from these facts and from the lease medium's `:lapsed` records (*Liveness*), as `dao.space.index` materializes a covered index, never from a table it keeps. The session-facts medium is a complete-history transport (`dao.stream.md`, Complete history); its growth is bounded only by OD-4's resolution, an open dependency.\n\n**Observation order.** A fact's `ok` is publication, not the router's observation of it, so the router never classifies from a projection that may lag. Each router pass reads the ingress first, then drains its session-facts and lineage-lease cursors to `blocked`, then classifies what it read. A token reaches the ingress only through a UI projected after its fact was accepted, so a fact precedes every request that names it, and a pass that has read the request has the fact to drain. The same holds for successor ids.\n\nThe drain proves this only on a direct handle: a reflection answers `blocked` while its request to the source is pending (`dao.stream.remote.md`, 2.4), which says nothing about the source. So **the router is composed on the peer that holds the session-facts medium, the lineage judge and the drivers**, and reads those two classification media directly; any other medium may be a reflection, which is the topology *Roles, not nodes* permits, so a driver separated from the HTTP boundary observes exchange grants and lapses over a reflection of the boundary's lease medium. A reflected ingress delays the read of a request, never its fact, so the proof stands. A router that read its facts through a reflection could not classify unknown at all; that composition is not permitted.\n\nThe router classifies by path; the boundary reads neither path nor token.\n\n| Path | Kind | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/` (no token) | begin | The root program starts a fresh lineage and answers a complete `text/html` document: `datastar.js`, actions carrying the root id, and `data-init=\"@get('/s/<token>')\"`. |\n| `/s/<token>` | subscribe | Opens the session's long-lived SSE exchange. Consumes nothing and advances nothing: the driver answers the head and a projection of the current occurrence, and every later patch goes here too. Reconnecting with the original URL is a new subscribe, resolved to the current occurrence however many inputs have passed. |\n| `/k/<token>`, token current | input | Consumed by the current occurrence (*Inputs, order and replay*). |\n| `/k/<token>`, token consumed | stale | Runs nothing. Answered by a projection of the current occurrence, then `:http/end`. |\n| any token of an ended or unknown lineage | begin | Halted, lapsed, or lost to a restart: answered as `/`. Unknown is judged only after the drain above. |\n\nSubscribe and stale requests are **re-projections**: the driver applies the projection function to the parked state without stepping it.\n\n### Signals\n\nA Datastar request carries the client's signals: in the `datastar` query parameter for GET and DELETE, in the JSON body otherwise. The boundary deposits them where HTTP put them, inside `:http/query` or `:http/body`, unread.\n\nDecoding is interpretation. `dao.star` supplies one pure projection from a request event to a signals map. The ingress tuple gains no slot for it, and signals become datoms only when an application interpreter derives them from the retained ingress stream (`datom.world.md`, Derive, don't persist).\n\nSignals are client-held state that moves, not shared state: each request carries an immutable snapshot, and the agent changes them only by appending a `datastar-patch-signals` effect. They are also the client's unverified claim. A signal is input to a continuation, never its state and never authority.\n\n### Inputs, order and replay\n\n**The token is the gate.** It is the request's only routing and idempotence key, and until ShiBi is load-bearing it is the sole gate on Mode 1 input: a pre-shared value in `dao.shibi.md`'s sense (owner ruling 2026-10-07).\n\n**Order is the ingress position.** Two inputs for one occurrence are not concurrent once deposited: one is earlier. Custody decides who consumes it. In a one-process composition the session's single driver is the only resumer; that exclusion is process-scoped and says so. Where more than one peer observes the ingress, resumption is a take, and a take is a lease on the occurrence (UCF §7.7).\n\n**One input, one successor.** Consuming an input advances occurrence `O` to its successor `O'` and appends the lineage fact. The reply goes to the exchange that carried the consumed input; patches for the session go to its open subscription exchanges, which the driver holds as its own interpreter state, built from the subscribe requests and terminal events routed to it.\n\n**Stale input is one-shot** (owner ruling 2026-10-07). A double click, a retry, a replayed URL, a second tab or the back button names an `O` that already has a successor. It runs nothing and is re-projected. The UI is a derivation, so answering twice is harmless, and a retry whose first reply was lost still converges. Mode 1 continuations are one-shot; multi-shot forks are deferred.\n\n**Effects.** Exactly-once needs a fenced writer into an enrolled consumer (UCF §7.7.5). Every other stream is at-least-once and declared so.\n\n### Liveness\n\nTwo leased resources, kept apart. Neither adds a clock to the wait for input: a parked continuation's `next` on its input is `blocked` until a request arrives, on a Tuesday or a Thursday.\n\n**The open exchange is leased.** The judge runs inside the peer that holds the HTTP boundary and is composed with the boundary's `close-exchange` as reclaim procedure and `open-exchanges` as its inventory (`dao.stream.http.md`, Inventory). For each `:http/request` it observes it grants unsolicited on a lease medium it writes: subject `{:http/exchange x}`, holder the egress medium's identity, a duration in `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`. Reclaim closes the exchange; the adapter deposits `:http/closed`, then the judge records `:lapsed`. A responder that keeps an exchange open observes its grant and appends `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal :dao.lease/lease L}` to the egress medium at under half the duration, from its own control flow, against its own tick stream. The judge and `egress-step` are peer observers of that medium; each ignores the other's facts. A one-shot responder answers inside the granted duration and renews nothing. An exchange nobody answers lapses by `:silence`; Datastar's reconnect opens a new one.\n\n**A gap is loss, and nothing is forwarded about it.** The judge, on an ingress `gap`, reconciles both ways against the inventory: an open exchange without a lease is granted afresh, and a leased exchange absent from the inventory is recorded `:lapsed` with cause `:policy`, so nothing is held unleased and nothing closed stays leased (inventory reconciliation; `dao.lease.md`'s reclaim-and-regrant is reserved for a lost ledger). The router forwards nothing about a gap: a request or terminal event lost in the evicted window is unrouted, and a forward whose `append!` answers anything but `ok` is the same loss; an unrouted request's exchange lapses by `:silence`. Drivers learn that an exchange is gone from the lease medium they already read for their grants: a `:lapsed` record for one of their exchanges drops it (`dao.lease.md`, Carriage). A driver whose cursor on any medium it reads answers `gap` forgets every subscription it holds and renews none; they lapse by `:silence` and close, and Datastar's reconnect subscribes again. Session media are declared at assembly like any deposit destination, evict-oldest with a capacity, and sized against the driver's lag.\n\n**Keep-alive is the responder's.** On its tick it may append an `:http/chunk` carrying an SSE comment line. That chunk is bytes for the wire, so the host discovers a dead peer by writing; the renewal is evidence for the judge. Neither stands in for the other. A peer's close or a failed write is deposited as `:http/closed`; the responder reads it as data and drops the exchange, and the judge ends the exchange lease with cause `:policy`.\n\n**The lineage is leased** (owner ruling 2026-10-07; the holder choice below is the architect's). A lineage nobody returns to is reclaimed through `dao.lease.md`, not by a timer. The peer holding the parked record is the grantor. When a lineage begins, its judge grants unsolicited on the lease medium: subject and holder `{:dao.star/lineage S}`, duration the composition's idle allowance, `:dao.lease/max` optional. Attribution is by envelope key: the session-facts medium is written only by session drivers, each stamping `:dao.star/lineage` on its facts, renewals and release, and the judge's resolver reads that key; until ShiBi, any writer of that medium can renew, the same exposure the exchange lease has. The driver observes its grant before consuming or projecting, renews at under half the duration from its own control flow, on each consumed input and on its tick while it holds an open subscription, and **stops at its bound**: past it, it consumes nothing and projects nothing, and an input arriving then is handed to the root program as begin. So a connected session is retained for as long as it stays connected, by design, bounded only by the cap; a disconnected one lapses after the idle allowance. The lease ends with the lineage: released by the driver on halt, lapsed by the judge on silence. Reclaim retires the runnable resource, the current parked record; the `:lapsed` record on the lease medium is the judge's own and is what ends the lineage for the router. History, successor facts and lease records are kept (`dao.lease.md`: a reclaim frees the resource, never the record). The subject is distinct from Mode 2's custody subject `{:yin.k/occurrence O}` on the arbitration medium. Leases are process-scoped until the composition settles the three durable duties `dao.lease.md` names.\n\nSizing: duration exceeds twice the renewal interval; the keep-alive interval stays under the shortest proxy or NAT idle timeout on the path; the ingress window exceeds router and judge lag and each session medium's window exceeds its driver's; a boundary restart loses sockets and ledger together, so its exchange inventory to reclaim is empty. A false lapse is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n\n---\n\n## Mode 2: The Fat Client (Mobile Continuations via UCF)\n\nStatus: proposed. Everything in this section waits on UCF version 1, fenced custody with a `dao.space` transactor as arbitration authority (`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` §7.7, 7.7.8). Version 0 is fork-only and cannot offer the exclusivity below.\n\nWhile `dao.star` champions the dumb terminal, it is not locked into it. Because `yin.vm` continuations are pure data, they can cross the network boundary to act as SPA-style fat clients for low-latency local interaction.\n\n### Flow\n\n1. **Exporting**: A `yin.vm` continuation on the server parks and enters the exporting state (UCF §7.7.4): its wait set is detached, so no poll or local resume can perform an effect while the value is in other hands. The lift produces a UCF handoff body, canonical and content-addressed, publishes it (*Handoff carrier*), and the driver records the offer on the arbitration medium.\n2. **Handoff**: The SSE patch carries the notice, not the body. A browser-side adapter deposits the notice on a browser-local stream that the browser's `yin.vm` reads; Datastar hands nothing to anyone.\n3. **Custody**: The browser `yin.vm` fetches the body by address over its WebSocket channel, proposes on the arbitration medium, and resumes only on observing its grant. Custody is exclusive because the lease is: the state moves to the browser rather than being shared with it, and the server's copy stays fenced.\n4. **Transparent Remote Access**: A continuation that needs a server-held stream uses the portable descriptors it carried and `attach!`es a reflection over WebSockets. Descriptors carry no authorization: the served entries are gated and leased for the migrated continuation (`dao.stream.remote.md`, sections 6 and 7). On channel loss a reflection answers `channel-gone` and reattachment is the continuation's own step on the same descriptor; a reclaimed entry answers `not-found` and is gone, not disconnected.\n5. **Migration Back**: The continuation re-serializes itself as a successor and offers it to the server, which admits it under *Admitting a continuation back*.\n\n### Handoff carrier\n\n**Architect ruling, not yet confirmed by the owner (2026-10-07).** The SSE patch carries a notice and the body travels by address over the browser's WebSocket channel. The notice is the body's content address, the arbitration descriptor, and a content coordinate `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests <remote descriptor> :dao.jing/answers <remote descriptor>}` (`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`, section 1).\n\nThe path: before the notice is appended, the lift publishes the body to the server's content store and appends the address to the session-facts medium as the lineage's export. The `requests` and `answers` entries are a pair served per lineage, so answers are not readable by other lineages, and gated with the lineage token as the pre-ShiBi credential; the gate's `verify` admits a `get` only for an address the lineage's own export facts name. The browser's fetch is the stepped content client, clock-free; it verifies the body by re-hashing (UCF §7.8, lower step 2). Three absences are distinct and all arrive as data: missing content is the service's `:jing/found? false`, a missing service is the remote `not-found` error, and a disallowed address is `:dao.stream/refused` at the gate. In each the browser holds nothing, and the exporter, a candidate like any other, proposes for itself under its own policy (UCF §7.7.4), so an unfetched offer strands nothing. Confidentiality is the gate's and the pair's: an address is a name, not an authorization.\n\nReasons: the body is canonical CBOR and SSE is text, so inlining means a second encoding; the egress medium is shared, and a body on it discloses the store slice to every observer, where a notice discloses a name; large values travel as content addresses (`dao.stream.md`, Granularity); and the browser `yin.vm` needs the channel anyway for custody and remote access, so the carrier adds no path.\n\nRejected: the body base64-encoded inside a `datastar-patch-signals` or element attribute (double encoding, disclosure on egress, Datastar's payload size); a second HTTP GET by address (a second boundary path for what the channel already carries).\n\n### Admitting a continuation back\n\nA continuation returning from the browser is admitted as untrusted code and state. It ran where nothing can attest to it, so it is treated as the controlled mode of `dao.space.security.md` treats any pushed interpreter.\n\n1. **Custody first** (UCF §7.7, unchanged). The successor must name, in `:yin.k/origin`, the lease the ledger holds live for that holder, and arrive on the holder's own medium. Otherwise `:yin.k/not-holder`.\n2. **Validate as hostile bytes** (UCF §7.8, lower steps 1 to 4), under a size bound and a step budget the composition declares.\n3. **Rebind authority; never read it from the value.** Lower into an isolated store. Primitives and FFI ops resolve from the receiver's registry, restricted to the set the composition delegated to this lineage at export. A stream marker attaches only if its identity is in that delegated set, or its channel is the returning peer's own. Anything else is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it.\n4. **No check survives the trip.** A decision the program made before export may have been skipped by a forged frame. Whatever must hold after return is enforced by a gate on a stream, or stays home in a continuation that never left and answers the migrated one over streams.\n\nThe test: a returned continuation can do nothing its principal could not do directly through the gated streams it was given.\n\n**Export is disclosure.** The lifted store slice and values are readable by the browser and by every peer the content gate admits. The composition inspects the computed closure before publishing.\n\n**Until effect handlers honor a delegated set** (`dao.space.security.md`, Honest Limits), migration back is admitted from allow-listed peers only (owner ruling 2026-10-07). ShiBi later plugs in at the same gates.\n\n---\n\n## Design Invariants Maintained\n\n- **No Shared Mutable State**: The UI is a pure projection (Mode 1), or custody of the continuation is held under an exclusive lease (Mode 2). State is never shared across the boundary; it moves, and the mover is fenced.\n- **Multiplexed Correlation**: Connections are not mapped 1:1 to streams. The HTTP boundary observes a shared egress stream and routes by exchange id. The cost is shared: an egress `gap` closes every open exchange, and a composition under that pressure shards the egress.\n- **Host Isolation**: The HTTP boundary is a dumb pipe that knows nothing of Datastar or `yin.vm` syntax. Datastar is a dumb renderer that knows nothing of backend state. Under Mode 2 a `yin.vm` runs beside it, not inside it.\n- **Data > Macros**: Compile-time slicing (Electric Clojure) is rejected. Network boundaries are crossed dynamically at runtime via reified data on explicit streams.\n- **No callbacks, no implicit control flow**: every arrival is a deposit, every departure is an effect, and every wait is a `blocked` answer at a cursor.\n- **No hidden state**: a driver's open subscriptions and the router's token index are interpreter state derived from media each reads with its own cursor, rebuilt from those media or honestly lost on a gap.\n\n## Deferred\n\n- **Multi-shot forks** (Arc's back button resuming a sibling branch): branch identity and subscription routing with a non-unique current are unspecified.\n- **Local projection** in Mode 2: the browser `yin.vm` appends the same Datastar-format effects to a browser-local egress stream read by a browser-side boundary adapter; same vocabulary, different boundary.\n- **Compaction of the session-facts medium**: waits on `dao.stream.md` OD-4.\n- **The content service as rules**: `dao.jing.content` is a plan section, not a spec; the notice above depends on it.\n- **An HTTP channel for peers without WebSocket**: `dao.stream.http.md`, Deferred.\n\n## Completion Criteria\n\n1. **Thin Client**: A `yin.vm` agent receives a Datastar input through the HTTP boundary's ingress stream, answers with `:http/head`, SSE-formatted `:http/chunk`s and `:http/end` on the egress stream, parks, and resumes on the next input for its current token. Also: a request with no token begins a lineage; a subscribe consumes nothing and a re-subscribe after several inputs receives the current projection; a stale token is re-projected; a closed socket is observed as `:http/closed`; a silent exchange lapses and closes; a lineage nobody returns to lapses, its parked record is retired and its facts remain; a request naming a just-published token is never answered as begin; after an ingress gap reconciliation leaves no exchange unleased and no closed exchange leased, and a driver stops renewing an exchange once it observes the lapse or its own gap; a renewal in flight may still count while the lease is live, and one observed after retirement does not revive it.\n2. **Fat Client Handoff**, conditional on UCF version 1 landing: a `yin.vm` agent exports under exclusive custody, the SSE patch carries the notice, a CLJS `yin.vm` in the browser fetches the body through the lineage's gated content pair, verifies it, obtains the grant, resumes, and reaches a server-held stream through a gated, leased reflection. Migration back from an allow-listed peer is admitted under *Admitting a continuation back*. The carrier shape is an architect ruling pending the owner's confirmation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792840587000-stream-engineer-dao-space-query-datomic-review.glm-5.3.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-24 11:44:21 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-24 18:44:21 +07\nCoding-Agent: glm\nSession-ID: 37072475-0422-4f8a-9fad-d7faa9b3b395\n\nRead-only review of the dao.space.query.datomic compatibility **plan** (no implementation exists) from the DaoStream & Distributed Protocol Engineer seat, at `f6856bbc`. Method: read the plan and all governing docs in full; verified every factual claim against `src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc`, `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`, the local `com.datomic/client-api:1.0.69` jar, and the repo's existing budget/deadline machinery. No files were edited and no tests were run.\n\n## Review-question verdicts\n\n**1. Execution machinery conformance (Phase 4) — PASSED WITH CHANGES (2 P2s below).** The design is implementable as specified without hidden control flow or ambient state, and it is not novel: it composes the repo's two ratified patterns. (a) *Step budgets as data* — `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:486-491` (step-event-budget ruling), `yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve.cljc:700-728` (`budgeted-reader`, exhaustion = `:dao.stream/blocked` data outcome, a fix for a prior \"P1 unbounded step\" gate), `dao/stream/remote.cljc:257-316` (`mirror-step` bounds), VM fuel (`yin/vm/semantic.cljc:142-155`). (b) *Deadline observations from an explicit driver* — `dao/stream/remote_route.cljc:468` (`:status :lost :reason :deadline` when `now` passes a deadline held as data), `dao/stream/remote.cljc:1157` (\"The link reads no clock: `now` is [supplied]\"), `dao/stream/ws.cljc:755-761` (\"this transport does not invent wall-clock time or a scheduler\"), `dao/stream/ws_project.cljc` `dial-step!` (\"Without `now` nothing expires\"). A driver called synchronously at explicit checkpoints is not a callback under the charter — control never inverts; it is the same terms as the existing explicit `:fns` map, and time-as-data is exactly `dao/lease.cljc:1552` and `yin/vm/linker.cljc:1956` (\"`fetch` takes no deadline; a composition that wants one holds a bound itself\"). Cross-host clock isolation is sufficient: no `.cljc` reader-conditional clock exists anywhere in `src/`; host mains mint readings and pass them down (`yin/repl/main.cljc:1313/1467/1601`), and `yin/vm/ucf/compose.cljc:187` already validates an injected clock seam. The between-checkpoints blocking caveat is acceptable and honestly stated; what is under-weighted is *granularity*, not the caveat itself (finding F1): a \"storage read\" is not only a caller fn — lazy B-tree slices fault one `jing/get` per node inside candidate production (`storage.cljc:56-65`, `btree.cljc` lazy `slice`), and the one blocking sleep-poll driver (`src/clj/dao/jing/content/driver.cljc:169-194`, JVM-only, throws on its own timeout) can overshoot a deadline unboundedly. The plan must define the step unit and the maximum overshoot. \"Legacy `q` drives the same machinery to completion\" is fine via an explicit never-expiring driver.\n\n**2. Adapter boundary discipline — PASSED.** The split holds in every phase: pagination is an adapter-side projection over the core's result value; `:timeout` maps to *generic* machinery (deadline as data) plus an adapter-owned driver, so no Client option name reaches core evaluation; Phase 2's blank-appending translation of 1-/2-position patterns is adapter-owned and verifiably preserves the index route (`query.cljc:1124` requires no `:rest` and exactly 3 fixed positions — the plan's \"do not translate to rest patterns\" reasoning is source-accurate). \"Eager complete evaluation before pagination\" is **not** mischaracterized: it is correctly listed as a documented difference with an explicit no-promise that `:limit` bounds evaluation, memory, or segment fetches, and the no-early-termination argument is true of the actual pipeline (grouping/aggregation/dedup happen after full evaluation, `relation-result` :1605-1640). What is owed is sizing guidance and a portable counter seam (F7).\n\n**3. Plan-claim accuracy — PASSED with two minor defects (F3, F8).** Spot-checks verified exact: `q` :1700 and `collect` :1761; get-else/missing? as index-aware special forms at :1273 (via `resolve-fact-index` + `select-probe`), **with** a lazy/eager parity test (`query_test.cljc:893-909`, \"get-else / missing? equivalence over lazy current view\" compares `eager-cur` vs `lazy-cur`); exact-arity matching :464 and the 3-fixed index-route check :1124; eager `into #{}` host-set construction :1615/:1631 with content-aware intermediate dedup; aggregate parser destructures only `[agg-sym arg]` :1549 (no parameterized-aggregate tests exist — consistent with the plan's \"add these\"); required-bound rule heads throw :1421-1425 and unbound head vars throw :1443-1447. The artifact corrections also verify against the local 1.0.69 jar: public `q` has exactly `([arg-map])`/`([query & args])` (`api.clj:410-467`), db-first exists only in the internal `Queryable` (`impl.clj:41-43`, shape `(q db arg-map)`), and `:ret` appears only as an output key in `:io-stats`/`:query-stats` envelopes — never an input mode. Ledger call sites at :232/:248/:265 confirm \"around 231–270\". One dispatch-side note: I count **72** deftests in `query_test.cljc` (grep at `f6856bbc`), not the dispatch's 74; no plan claim depends on the count.\n\n**4. Lifecycle and input ownership — PASSED.** Verified in source: `q`/`collect`/`realize-db-value!` open and close nothing (:1700-1706, :394-396); `open-published!`/`close-published!` are caller moves, and a failed open closes the store it opened (:268-281). Result values carry only projected data (`relation-result` projects values; dbs live in bindings, never in results), so no adapter path can retain an open index handle in a *result*. Timeout path: throw-after-checkpoint opens nothing, closes nothing — borrowing preserved on success and failure. The one wrinkle is ex-data retention (F6): existing throws embed full `:binding` maps (:1244-1245, :1268-1269), which carry `::dbs` → restored trees and the delayed rows over the caller's store handle.\n\n**5. Omissions and risks — 4 of my findings are seat-owned omissions (F2, F4, F6, F7); the rest check clean.** No observer/snapshot interaction risk: `snapshot` is a pre-`q` caller move and `dao.stream.observe/step` (single-step, no budget/time param, effect-based) is untouched by the plan. No transport-level concern for the host driver (local adapter only; and because deadlines and steps stay data, the design composes with the OD-5 stepped rule if queries ever run remotely — worth one sentence in the compat doc). The step budget incidentally bounds rule divergence beyond the cycle guard (F9).\n\n## Findings (severity-ordered)\n\n**F1. P2 | Plan §Phase 4 (`execution.cljc` spec) | explicit causality / no implicit control flow |** Step granularity and forcing discipline are unspecified against a *lazy* evaluator: `eval-clause` composes lazy seqs (:1455-1469), pattern clauses produce lazy `keep`s (:1126-1132), candidates stream from a lazy `bt/slice`, and nothing is forced until `relation-result` (:1612-1640) — node faults (one blocking `jing/get` each) happen inside candidate production. \"Bounded steps\" is unfalsifiable until the plan says what one step is, when observations fire relative to lazy realization, and the maximum deadline overshoot. **Correction:** pin the unit (recommend one candidate binding produced during the forced pass, plus observations at each phase boundary parsing/realization/join/recursion/aggregation/output), and adopt the repo's documented-overshoot discipline (\"expiry is observed within one tick of the deadline\", `remote_channel.cljc:63-69`) — here: *within one storage read of the deadline*, by checkpointing around each range scan (`select-datoms`, :1042-1044), not merely per clause. *Plan ambiguity, not an architectural defect.*\n\n**F2. P2 | Plan §Phase 4 | no shared mutable state; one-invocation ownership |** Execution state must thread through *nested* evaluation. `eval-where` recurses via `not`/`not-join`/`or`/`or-join`/rules (:1455-1469); rule eval already threads `::active-rules` through `ctx` (:1413-1416) as the seam. The plan never says sub-evaluations share the invocation's state; a fresh state per nested `eval-where` lets budget escape exactly where work is least visible. **Correction:** one invocation-scoped state riding the explicit `ctx` threading (the `::active-rules` precedent), covering nested evaluation, with no dynamic-var or atom escape hatch. *Plan ambiguity.*\n\n**F3. P2 | Plan §\"Existing callers needing regression protection\" | downstream-caller completeness |** `dao.space.dht` is omitted: `src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:1498` runs `(apply query/q query (query/current (db node manifest-address)) inputs)` — a `q` over a lazily-restored published index, precisely the path Phase 4 refactors. (`dao.space.transact` also exercises `match`/`current` over relations at :60/:80/:282/:304/:334/:365 if the machinery touches realization.) **Correction:** add `dao.space.dht` (and transact's realize-path uses) to the Phase 5 downstream-caller gates. *Plan omission, factual.*\n\n**F4. P2 | Plan §Phase 4 acceptance criteria | cross-host test discipline (this seat) |** Timeout-during-scans/joins/rules acceptance tests are unconstrained; wall-clock-sleeping timeout tests flake on this repo's lanes (Node slow lane ~60 min, Dart multi-shard log artifacts, poll-until flake history). **Correction:** semantics tests inject a deterministic fake driver (expiry after N observations — the repo already validates injected clocks as data, `ucf/compose.cljc:187`); wall-clock appears only in one generously-margined smoke test per host; no sub-second real timeouts in lanes. *Plan omission.*\n\n**F5. P3 | Plan §Timeout call | no hidden global state |** Clock source and monotonicity unpinned. **Correction:** name the driver seam (zero-arg `now`-fn, or an absolute deadline minted at entry as `remote.cljc:379-397` does, compared as data at checkpoints); forbid a namespace-level default clock var in `execution.cljc`; note `js/Date.now`/`DateTime.now` are wall clocks (NTP jumps stretch timeouts) — prefer a monotonic source where the host offers one. *Plan ambiguity.*\n\n**F6. P3 | Plan §Errors / Phase 4 timeout category | borrowed-input lifecycle on failure |** Existing throws embed `:binding` maps (:1244-1245, :1268-1269) carrying `::dbs` → restored trees and delayed rows over the caller's store handle; a timeout `ex-info` built the same way pins the caller's materialized state inside the error. Not a lifecycle break, but it defeats the failure-path borrowing expectation and bloats error data. **Correction:** timeout/eval error categories carry minimal projected context (clause, var, step count), never `::dbs` or full bindings. *Plan omission.*\n\n**F7. P3 | Plan §Decision 1 / §Conformance strategy | memory bounds; observability |** The eager-before-pagination difference is honestly labeled, but the compat spec owes a sizing statement (an unselective 3-fixed clause takes the `(seq (:eavt idx))` full-walk fallback, :526, draining the whole restored EAVT into a host set before `:limit` applies). Also, \"segment-read counters\" do not exist: the only harness is the test-side `counting-content-store` + `with-redefs` (skipped on ClojureDart, `query_test.cljc:999-1002`); the sole production seam is the no-op `IStorage/-accessed` (`storage.cljc:68`). **Correction:** add sizing guidance to `dao.space.query.datomic-compat.md`; build the counter on `-accessed` (or an explicit wrapper store) so all three hosts count. *Minor omission.*\n\n**F8. P3 | Plan §Corrections table | claim accuracy |** The final-set-collapse limitation is cited at `query.cljc:1597`; the documenting docstring is `relation-result` at :1605-1611 (:1597 is `project-element`'s `resolve-fact-index`). Every other line citation checked exact (see Q3). **Correction:** fix the citation. *Minor accuracy defect.*\n\n**F9. P3 | Plan §Phase 3 × Phase 4 interaction | termination of recursive rules |** The cycle guard fails only calls with identical resolved args (:1412-1416); a rule body generating unboundedly fresh values (fn-clause successors) diverges without repeating a call key — Phase 3 tabling addresses completeness, not termination. **Correction:** one sentence in the compat doc naming Phase 4's step budget as the termination backstop for the accepted recursion class. *Intentionally-adjacent, recommend documenting.*\n\n**F10. P3 | Plan §Timeout call | exhaustion semantics precedent |** Every existing budget/deadline mechanism in the repo answers exhaustion as *data* (`:dao.stream/blocked`, parked registers, `:lost`/`:deadline`); the query timeout will be a structured exception (\"no partial success\"). That is defensible — `q` is an interpreter above streams, its existing error style is `ex-info`, and the compat target takes `:timeout` as a request error — but the divergence from the data-outcome precedent should be named in Phase 0 so the implementation reviewer does not read it as an invariant breach. *Documentation.*\n\nAlso noted, not a finding: `com.datomic/client-api` is not declared in `deps.edn` (only `com.datomic/peer 1.0.7364`); the plan never claims otherwise, but Phase 0's oracle runner under `src/dev/` will need client-api added at dev scope.\n\n## Properties that PASSED review\n\n- Adapter/core split with no layer collapsing: no Client option name reaches core evaluation in any phase; the timeout mechanism is generic (deadline as data) with the driver adapter-owned.\n- Stream primacy of the timeout design: no callbacks (synchronous driver reads at explicit checkpoints), no background tasks, no global clock state; composes with the OD-5 stepped rule for any future remote execution.\n- No hidden global state / no ambient RNG: the `rand`/`sample` exclusion on state-boundary grounds is correct; execution progress and deadlines are per-invocation.\n- Input borrowing on success and failure: `q`/`collect` open and close nothing (source-verified); pagination is a pure output projection; results carry no source references.\n- Lazy published-index realization preserved: the planning-never-forces property (:1472-1485) is untouched; abbreviated-pattern translation stays on the index route.\n- Plan-claim accuracy: all substantive source and artifact claims verified (two minor defects, F3/F8); exclusions and deferrals are honestly separated.\n- Conformance strategy: frozen fixtures with provenance, no self-regenerating expectations, shared-logical-fixture dual loaders, gate ladder with `bb test:clj`/`:cljs`/`:cljd`.\n\nVerdict: **ENDORSE WITH CHANGES** — the plan is architecturally conformant and factually accurate, but Phase 4 must pin step granularity/forcing discipline and nested-state threading (F1/F2), and the caller-protection list must add `dao.space.dht` (F3), before implementation is commissioned.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/design/datom.world.md; cat docs/agents/roles/architect.md; rg -n 'query/|realize' src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc; sed -n '1595,1620p' src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/design/datom.world.md; cat docs/agents/roles/architect.md; rg -n 'query/|realize' src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc; sed -n '1595,1620p' src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n---\ndescription: Lead System Architect role definition for datom.world\n---\n\n# ROLE: Lead System Architect\n\n## Domain Scope\n\n- Foundational axioms, architectural invariants, and design contracts\n- Subsystem boundaries, interpreters, host isolation, and explicit causality\n- Cross-platform architecture, migration strategy, and compatibility boundaries\n- ADR coherence, domain vocabulary, and theoretical grounding where useful\n- Architecture review and sign-off for high-risk or cross-cutting changes\n\nThis role owns no permanent file list. Each task defines the artifacts it may\ninspect or change and any permitted expansion.\n\n## Core Responsibilities\n\n1. **Protect Invariants**: Strictly enforce the 6 non-negotiable invariants (no hidden global state, no implicit control flow, no raw callbacks, no shared mutable state, no layer collapsing, no assumed graphs).\n2. **Decompose Complexity**: When new requirements emerge, decompose them into append-only streams, explicit interpreters, and immutable datom representations.\n3. **Malleability Governance**: Ensure architecture adheres to bounded complexity, loose coupling, and high cohesion as defined in [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../malleability.md).\n4. **Lineage Alignment**: Keep designs grounded in Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp architectural lineage.\n\n## Delegation Prompt Template\n\n```text\nCreated-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\nCreated-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\n\n# Task: <Task Name>\n\nRole: Lead System Architect\n\nImplementers:\n- Model: <model-name> | Assigned: <local timestamp> | Status: active | Rationale: <why>\n\nPerform a read-only architecture review of <design-change>.\n\nRead first:\n- docs/design/datom.world.md\n- <target-design-file>\n- <relevant-implementation-boundary>\n- <relevant-contract-tests>\n\nEvaluate foundational invariants, ownership boundaries, explicit state and\ncontrol flow, concurrency and linearization, dynamic extension, host isolation,\nCLJ/CLJS/CLJD portability, migration risk, completion criteria, and design\ncontradictions. Distinguish architectural defects from implementation gaps or\nintentionally deferred work. Do not edit files.\n\nBegin the final response exactly with:\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\n\nThen report: severity | file:line | invariant/evidence | recommended correction.\nAlso confirm the requested properties that passed review.\n```\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:60:      (let [res (query/match (query/current (query/relation base-datoms))\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:80:                            (query/match (query/current (query/relation\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:282:                                 (query/match (query/current (query/relation\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:304:                              (query/match (query/current (query/relation\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:334:              (let [existing (query/match (query/current (query/relation\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:365:                    (let [existing (query/match (query/current (query/relation\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:4:   by any walk, and query a loaded covered index with `dao.space.query/q`\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:41:     read-manifest` and `restored-indexes` (`dao.space.query/\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:1490:  (query/published-db (local node) manifest-address))\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:1494:  \"`dao.space.query/q` of `query` over the current view of the loaded\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:1497:  (query/collect\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:1498:    (apply query/q query (query/current (db node manifest-address)) inputs)))\n  (if (:pull-var element)\n    (let [eid (get b (:pull-var element))\n          idx (resolve-fact-index b '$)]\n      (when-not idx\n        (throw (ex-info \"pull find element requires a bound $ source\"\n                        {:element element})))\n      (pull-idx idx eid (:pull-pattern element)))\n    (get b (:var element))))\n\n\n(defn- relation-result\n  \"The q result: a host set of find tuples. Rows, groups and tuples are\n   deduplicated kind-strictly (content-distinct, content-key) before the\n   set is built. The set itself still compares members with host =, so on\n   a host where two content-distinct tuples are host = and hash alike (the\n   JVM for 1.0M/1.00M and 0.0/-0.0, Dart for 0.0/-0.0) they remain one\n   member of the returned set.\"\n  [find with bindings]\n  (let [elements (mapv parse-find-element find)]\n    (if (not-any? :agg elements)\n      (into #{}\n            (content-distinct (map (fn [b] (mapv #(project-element % b) elements))\n                                   bindings)))\n      (let [grouping-vars (filterv some?\n                                   (mapv #(or (:var %) (:pull-var %)) elements))\n            ;; proj-vars are query variable symbols, which host distinct\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '38,175p' docs/design/dao.star.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '38,175p' docs/design/dao.star.md\"","aggregated_output":"### Sessions and request kinds\n\nA **lineage** is one session's chain of occurrences: a root and every successor a consumed input produced. **The token is the occurrence id**, minted unguessable by the driver; the lineage's identity is its root's id. Every projected UI carries the id of the occurrence that rendered it. The lineage's **current** occurrence is the one with no successor.\n\nThe driver appends one fact per transition to the **session-facts** medium, and appends it with `:dao.stream/ok` before any UI carrying the new id is projected:\n\n```clojure\n{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/occurrence S}                            ; root\n{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/occurrence O2 :dao.star/predecessor O1}  ; consumption\n{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/ended :halted}                           ; the program finished\n```\n\nThe router materializes occurrence → lineage → current from these facts and from the lease medium's `:lapsed` records (*Liveness*), as `dao.space.index` materializes a covered index, never from a table it keeps. The session-facts medium is a complete-history transport (`dao.stream.md`, Complete history); its growth is bounded only by OD-4's resolution, an open dependency.\n\n**Observation order.** A fact's `ok` is publication, not the router's observation of it, so the router never classifies from a projection that may lag. Each router pass reads the ingress first, then drains its session-facts and lineage-lease cursors to `blocked`, then classifies what it read. A token reaches the ingress only through a UI projected after its fact was accepted, so a fact precedes every request that names it, and a pass that has read the request has the fact to drain. The same holds for successor ids.\n\nThe drain proves this only on a direct handle: a reflection answers `blocked` while its request to the source is pending (`dao.stream.remote.md`, 2.4), which says nothing about the source. So **the router is composed on the peer that holds the session-facts medium, the lineage judge and the drivers**, and reads those two classification media directly; any other medium may be a reflection, which is the topology *Roles, not nodes* permits, so a driver separated from the HTTP boundary observes exchange grants and lapses over a reflection of the boundary's lease medium. A reflected ingress delays the read of a request, never its fact, so the proof stands. A router that read its facts through a reflection could not classify unknown at all; that composition is not permitted.\n\nThe router classifies by path; the boundary reads neither path nor token.\n\n| Path | Kind | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/` (no token) | begin | The root program starts a fresh lineage and answers a complete `text/html` document: `datastar.js`, actions carrying the root id, and `data-init=\"@get('/s/<token>')\"`. |\n| `/s/<token>` | subscribe | Opens the session's long-lived SSE exchange. Consumes nothing and advances nothing: the driver answers the head and a projection of the current occurrence, and every later patch goes here too. Reconnecting with the original URL is a new subscribe, resolved to the current occurrence however many inputs have passed. |\n| `/k/<token>`, token current | input | Consumed by the current occurrence (*Inputs, order and replay*). |\n| `/k/<token>`, token consumed | stale | Runs nothing. Answered by a projection of the current occurrence, then `:http/end`. |\n| any token of an ended or unknown lineage | begin | Halted, lapsed, or lost to a restart: answered as `/`. Unknown is judged only after the drain above. |\n\nSubscribe and stale requests are **re-projections**: the driver applies the projection function to the parked state without stepping it.\n\n### Signals\n\nA Datastar request carries the client's signals: in the `datastar` query parameter for GET and DELETE, in the JSON body otherwise. The boundary deposits them where HTTP put them, inside `:http/query` or `:http/body`, unread.\n\nDecoding is interpretation. `dao.star` supplies one pure projection from a request event to a signals map. The ingress tuple gains no slot for it, and signals become datoms only when an application interpreter derives them from the retained ingress stream (`datom.world.md`, Derive, don't persist).\n\nSignals are client-held state that moves, not shared state: each request carries an immutable snapshot, and the agent changes them only by appending a `datastar-patch-signals` effect. They are also the client's unverified claim. A signal is input to a continuation, never its state and never authority.\n\n### Inputs, order and replay\n\n**The token is the gate.** It is the request's only routing and idempotence key, and until ShiBi is load-bearing it is the sole gate on Mode 1 input: a pre-shared value in `dao.shibi.md`'s sense (owner ruling 2026-10-07).\n\n**Order is the ingress position.** Two inputs for one occurrence are not concurrent once deposited: one is earlier. Custody decides who consumes it. In a one-process composition the session's single driver is the only resumer; that exclusion is process-scoped and says so. Where more than one peer observes the ingress, resumption is a take, and a take is a lease on the occurrence (UCF §7.7).\n\n**One input, one successor.** Consuming an input advances occurrence `O` to its successor `O'` and appends the lineage fact. The reply goes to the exchange that carried the consumed input; patches for the session go to its open subscription exchanges, which the driver holds as its own interpreter state, built from the subscribe requests and terminal events routed to it.\n\n**Stale input is one-shot** (owner ruling 2026-10-07). A double click, a retry, a replayed URL, a second tab or the back button names an `O` that already has a successor. It runs nothing and is re-projected. The UI is a derivation, so answering twice is harmless, and a retry whose first reply was lost still converges. Mode 1 continuations are one-shot; multi-shot forks are deferred.\n\n**Effects.** Exactly-once needs a fenced writer into an enrolled consumer (UCF §7.7.5). Every other stream is at-least-once and declared so.\n\n### Liveness\n\nTwo leased resources, kept apart. Neither adds a clock to the wait for input: a parked continuation's `next` on its input is `blocked` until a request arrives, on a Tuesday or a Thursday.\n\n**The open exchange is leased.** The judge runs inside the peer that holds the HTTP boundary and is composed with the boundary's `close-exchange` as reclaim procedure and `open-exchanges` as its inventory (`dao.stream.http.md`, Inventory). For each `:http/request` it observes it grants unsolicited on a lease medium it writes: subject `{:http/exchange x}`, holder the egress medium's identity, a duration in `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`. Reclaim closes the exchange; the adapter deposits `:http/closed`, then the judge records `:lapsed`. A responder that keeps an exchange open observes its grant and appends `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal :dao.lease/lease L}` to the egress medium at under half the duration, from its own control flow, against its own tick stream. The judge and `egress-step` are peer observers of that medium; each ignores the other's facts. A one-shot responder answers inside the granted duration and renews nothing. An exchange nobody answers lapses by `:silence`; Datastar's reconnect opens a new one.\n\n**A gap is loss, and nothing is forwarded about it.** The judge, on an ingress `gap`, reconciles both ways against the inventory: an open exchange without a lease is granted afresh, and a leased exchange absent from the inventory is recorded `:lapsed` with cause `:policy`, so nothing is held unleased and nothing closed stays leased (inventory reconciliation; `dao.lease.md`'s reclaim-and-regrant is reserved for a lost ledger). The router forwards nothing about a gap: a request or terminal event lost in the evicted window is unrouted, and a forward whose `append!` answers anything but `ok` is the same loss; an unrouted request's exchange lapses by `:silence`. Drivers learn that an exchange is gone from the lease medium they already read for their grants: a `:lapsed` record for one of their exchanges drops it (`dao.lease.md`, Carriage). A driver whose cursor on any medium it reads answers `gap` forgets every subscription it holds and renews none; they lapse by `:silence` and close, and Datastar's reconnect subscribes again. Session media are declared at assembly like any deposit destination, evict-oldest with a capacity, and sized against the driver's lag.\n\n**Keep-alive is the responder's.** On its tick it may append an `:http/chunk` carrying an SSE comment line. That chunk is bytes for the wire, so the host discovers a dead peer by writing; the renewal is evidence for the judge. Neither stands in for the other. A peer's close or a failed write is deposited as `:http/closed`; the responder reads it as data and drops the exchange, and the judge ends the exchange lease with cause `:policy`.\n\n**The lineage is leased** (owner ruling 2026-10-07; the holder choice below is the architect's). A lineage nobody returns to is reclaimed through `dao.lease.md`, not by a timer. The peer holding the parked record is the grantor. When a lineage begins, its judge grants unsolicited on the lease medium: subject and holder `{:dao.star/lineage S}`, duration the composition's idle allowance, `:dao.lease/max` optional. Attribution is by envelope key: the session-facts medium is written only by session drivers, each stamping `:dao.star/lineage` on its facts, renewals and release, and the judge's resolver reads that key; until ShiBi, any writer of that medium can renew, the same exposure the exchange lease has. The driver observes its grant before consuming or projecting, renews at under half the duration from its own control flow, on each consumed input and on its tick while it holds an open subscription, and **stops at its bound**: past it, it consumes nothing and projects nothing, and an input arriving then is handed to the root program as begin. So a connected session is retained for as long as it stays connected, by design, bounded only by the cap; a disconnected one lapses after the idle allowance. The lease ends with the lineage: released by the driver on halt, lapsed by the judge on silence. Reclaim retires the runnable resource, the current parked record; the `:lapsed` record on the lease medium is the judge's own and is what ends the lineage for the router. History, successor facts and lease records are kept (`dao.lease.md`: a reclaim frees the resource, never the record). The subject is distinct from Mode 2's custody subject `{:yin.k/occurrence O}` on the arbitration medium. Leases are process-scoped until the composition settles the three durable duties `dao.lease.md` names.\n\nSizing: duration exceeds twice the renewal interval; the keep-alive interval stays under the shortest proxy or NAT idle timeout on the path; the ingress window exceeds router and judge lag and each session medium's window exceeds its driver's; a boundary restart loses sockets and ledger together, so its exchange inventory to reclaim is empty. A false lapse is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n\n---\n\n## Mode 2: The Fat Client (Mobile Continuations via UCF)\n\nStatus: proposed. Everything in this section waits on UCF version 1, fenced custody with a `dao.space` transactor as arbitration authority (`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` §7.7, 7.7.8). Version 0 is fork-only and cannot offer the exclusivity below.\n\nWhile `dao.star` champions the dumb terminal, it is not locked into it. Because `yin.vm` continuations are pure data, they can cross the network boundary to act as SPA-style fat clients for low-latency local interaction.\n\n### Flow\n\n1. **Exporting**: A `yin.vm` continuation on the server parks and enters the exporting state (UCF §7.7.4): its wait set is detached, so no poll or local resume can perform an effect while the value is in other hands. The lift produces a UCF handoff body, canonical and content-addressed, publishes it (*Handoff carrier*), and the driver records the offer on the arbitration medium.\n2. **Handoff**: The SSE patch carries the notice, not the body. A browser-side adapter deposits the notice on a browser-local stream that the browser's `yin.vm` reads; Datastar hands nothing to anyone.\n3. **Custody**: The browser `yin.vm` fetches the body by address over its WebSocket channel, proposes on the arbitration medium, and resumes only on observing its grant. Custody is exclusive because the lease is: the state moves to the browser rather than being shared with it, and the server's copy stays fenced.\n4. **Transparent Remote Access**: A continuation that needs a server-held stream uses the portable descriptors it carried and `attach!`es a reflection over WebSockets. Descriptors carry no authorization: the served entries are gated and leased for the migrated continuation (`dao.stream.remote.md`, sections 6 and 7). On channel loss a reflection answers `channel-gone` and reattachment is the continuation's own step on the same descriptor; a reclaimed entry answers `not-found` and is gone, not disconnected.\n5. **Migration Back**: The continuation re-serializes itself as a successor and offers it to the server, which admits it under *Admitting a continuation back*.\n\n### Handoff carrier\n\n**Architect ruling, not yet confirmed by the owner (2026-10-07).** The SSE patch carries a notice and the body travels by address over the browser's WebSocket channel. The notice is the body's content address, the arbitration descriptor, and a content coordinate `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests <remote descriptor> :dao.jing/answers <remote descriptor>}` (`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`, section 1).\n\nThe path: before the notice is appended, the lift publishes the body to the server's content store and appends the address to the session-facts medium as the lineage's export. The `requests` and `answers` entries are a pair served per lineage, so answers are not readable by other lineages, and gated with the lineage token as the pre-ShiBi credential; the gate's `verify` admits a `get` only for an address the lineage's own export facts name. The browser's fetch is the stepped content client, clock-free; it verifies the body by re-hashing (UCF §7.8, lower step 2). Three absences are distinct and all arrive as data: missing content is the service's `:jing/found? false`, a missing service is the remote `not-found` error, and a disallowed address is `:dao.stream/refused` at the gate. In each the browser holds nothing, and the exporter, a candidate like any other, proposes for itself under its own policy (UCF §7.7.4), so an unfetched offer strands nothing. Confidentiality is the gate's and the pair's: an address is a name, not an authorization.\n\nReasons: the body is canonical CBOR and SSE is text, so inlining means a second encoding; the egress medium is shared, and a body on it discloses the store slice to every observer, where a notice discloses a name; large values travel as content addresses (`dao.stream.md`, Granularity); and the browser `yin.vm` needs the channel anyway for custody and remote access, so the carrier adds no path.\n\nRejected: the body base64-encoded inside a `datastar-patch-signals` or element attribute (double encoding, disclosure on egress, Datastar's payload size); a second HTTP GET by address (a second boundary path for what the channel already carries).\n\n### Admitting a continuation back\n\nA continuation returning from the browser is admitted as untrusted code and state. It ran where nothing can attest to it, so it is treated as the controlled mode of `dao.space.security.md` treats any pushed interpreter.\n\n1. **Custody first** (UCF §7.7, unchanged). The successor must name, in `:yin.k/origin`, the lease the ledger holds live for that holder, and arrive on the holder's own medium. Otherwise `:yin.k/not-holder`.\n2. **Validate as hostile bytes** (UCF §7.8, lower steps 1 to 4), under a size bound and a step budget the composition declares.\n3. **Rebind authority; never read it from the value.** Lower into an isolated store. Primitives and FFI ops resolve from the receiver's registry, restricted to the set the composition delegated to this lineage at export. A stream marker attaches only if its identity is in that delegated set, or its channel is the returning peer's own. Anything else is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it.\n4. **No check survives the trip.** A decision the program made before export may have been skipped by a forged frame. Whatever must hold after return is enforced by a gate on a stream, or stays home in a continuation that never left and answers the migrated one over streams.\n\nThe test: a returned continuation can do nothing its principal could not do directly through the gated streams it was given.\n\n**Export is disclosure.** The lifted store slice and values are readable by the browser and by every peer the content gate admits. The composition inspects the computed closure before publishing.\n\n**Until effect handlers honor a delegated set** (`dao.space.security.md`, Honest Limits), migration back is admitted from allow-listed peers only (owner ruling 2026-10-07). ShiBi later plugs in at the same gates.\n\n---\n\n## Design Invariants Maintained\n\n- **No Shared Mutable State**: The UI is a pure projection (Mode 1), or custody of the continuation is held under an exclusive lease (Mode 2). State is never shared across the boundary; it moves, and the mover is fenced.\n- **Multiplexed Correlation**: Connections are not mapped 1:1 to streams. The HTTP boundary observes a shared egress stream and routes by exchange id. The cost is shared: an egress `gap` closes every open exchange, and a composition under that pressure shards the egress.\n- **Host Isolation**: The HTTP boundary is a dumb pipe that knows nothing of Datastar or `yin.vm` syntax. Datastar is a dumb renderer that knows nothing of backend state. Under Mode 2 a `yin.vm` runs beside it, not inside it.\n- **Data > Macros**: Compile-time slicing (Electric Clojure) is rejected. Network boundaries are crossed dynamically at runtime via reified data on explicit streams.\n- **No callbacks, no implicit control flow**: every arrival is a deposit, every departure is an effect, and every wait is a `blocked` answer at a cursor.\n- **No hidden state**: a driver's open subscriptions and the router's token index are interpreter state derived from media each reads with its own cursor, rebuilt from those media or honestly lost on a gap.\n\n## Deferred\n\n- **Multi-shot forks** (Arc's back button resuming a sibling branch): branch identity and subscription routing with a non-unique current are unspecified.\n- **Local projection** in Mode 2: the browser `yin.vm` appends the same Datastar-format effects to a browser-local egress stream read by a browser-side boundary adapter; same vocabulary, different boundary.\n- **Compaction of the session-facts medium**: waits on `dao.stream.md` OD-4.\n- **The content service as rules**: `dao.jing.content` is a plan section, not a spec; the notice above depends on it.\n- **An HTTP channel for peers without WebSocket**: `dao.stream.http.md`, Deferred.\n\n## Completion Criteria\n\n1. **Thin Client**: A `yin.vm` agent receives a Datastar input through the HTTP boundary's ingress stream, answers with `:http/head`, SSE-formatted `:http/chunk`s and `:http/end` on the egress stream, parks, and resumes on the next input for its current token. Also: a request with no token begins a lineage; a subscribe consumes nothing and a re-subscribe after several inputs receives the current projection; a stale token is re-projected; a closed socket is observed as `:http/closed`; a silent exchange lapses and closes; a lineage nobody returns to lapses, its parked record is retired and its facts remain; a request naming a just-published token is never answered as begin; after an ingress gap reconciliation leaves no exchange unleased and no closed exchange leased, and a driver stops renewing an exchange once it observes the lapse or its own gap; a renewal in flight may still count while the lease is live, and one observed after retirement does not revive it.\n2. **Fat Client Handoff**, conditional on UCF version 1 landing: a `yin.vm` agent exports under exclusive custody, the SSE patch carries the notice, a CLJS `yin.vm` in the browser fetches the body through the lineage's gated content pair, verifies it, obtains the grant, resumes, and reaches a server-held stream through a gated, leased reflection. Migration back from an allow-listed peer is admitted under *Admitting a continuation back*. The carrier shape is an architect ruling pending the owner's confirmation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"unzip -p /Users/sto/.m2/repository/com/datomic/client-api/1.0.69/client-api-1.0.69.jar datomic/client/api.clj | sed -n '16,42p;410,468p'; sed -n '1,40p' docs/design/dao.space.query.md; sed -n '120,135p' docs/agents/build-n-test.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"unzip -p /Users/sto/.m2/repository/com/datomic/client-api/1.0.69/client-api-1.0.69.jar datomic/client/api.clj | sed -n '16,42p;410,468p'; sed -n '1,40p' docs/design/dao.space.query.md; sed -n '120,135p' docs/agents/build-n-test.md\"","aggregated_output":"(ns datomic.client.api\n  \"Synchronous client library for interacting with Datomic.\n\nThis namespace is a wrapper for datomic.client.api.async.\n\nFunctions in this namespace that communicate with a separate\nprocess take an arg-map with the following optional keys:\n\n  :timeout   Timeout in msec.\n\nFunctions that support offset and limit take the following\nadditional optional keys:\n\n  :offset    Number of results to omit from the beginning\n             of the returned data.\n  :limit     Maximum total number of results to return.\n             Specify -1 for no limit. Defaults to -1 for q\n             and to 1000 for all other APIs.\n\nFunctions that return datoms return values of a type that supports\nindexed (count/nth) access of [e a v t added] as well as\nlookup (keyword) access via :e :a :v :t :added.\n\nAll errors are reported via ex-info exceptions, with map contents\nas specified by cognitect.anomalies.\nSee https://github.com/cognitect-labs/anomalies.\"\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [sync])\n(defn q\n  \"Performs the query described by query and args:\n\n  :query  The query to perform: a map, list, or string (see below).\n  :args   Data sources for the query, e.g. database values\n          retrieved from a call to db, and/or rules.\n\n  The query list form is [:find ?var1 ?var2 ...\n                          :with ?var3 ...\n                          :in $src1 $src2 ...\n                          :where clause1 clause2 ...]\n\n  :find  specifies the tuples to be returned\n  :with  is optional, and names vars to be kept in the aggrgation set\n         but not returned\n  :in    is optional. Omitting ':in ...' is the same as specifying\n         ':in $'\n  :where limits the result returned\n\nFor a complete description of the query syntax, see\nhttps://docs.datomic.com/cloud/query/query-data-reference.html.\n\nReturns a collection of tuples.\n\nThe arity-1 version takes :query and :args in arg-map, which\nallows additional options for :offset, :limit, and :timeout. See\nnamespace doc.\n\nYou can get information about the I/O reads performed by a query\nwith io-stats. To request io-stats, add :io-context (a qualified\nkeyword) to the arg-map you use to call query. Query will return a map\nwith\n\n :ret                the result of the query\n :io-stats           io-stats for the query\n\nThe io-stats map includes:\n\n :io-context         the io-context passed in\n :api                :tx-with\n :api-ms             msec to peform the API call\n :reads              breakout of reads by cache tier and index sort\n :nested             breakout iff nested queries set :io-context\n\nSee https://docs.datomic.com/cloud/api/io-stats.html.\n\nYou can request query-stats by adding :query-stats true to the map you\nuse to call query. Query will return a map with\n\n :ret                   the result of the query\n :query-stats           query-stats for the query\n\nSee https://docs.datomic.com/cloud/api/query-stats.html.\"\n  ([arg-map]\n     (impl/call-q arg-map impl/q))\n  ([query & args]\n     (q {:query query :args args})))\n\n\n# dao.space.query — The Reader-Side Index Consumer\n\nStatus: implemented, on DaoStream v2. Every database input is a **value**:\na relation value (`query/relation`, `query/entity-map-relation`), a datom\nview over one (`current`, `history`), or a published covered index the caller\nopened (`open-published!`). `q` opens nothing and closes nothing. A live\nDaoStream enters only through `snapshot`, the single interpreter over\n`dao.stream.observe/step`. The index realization is owned by\n`dao.space.index`, and `match` / `q` / `pull` run over one evaluator on every\nplatform, returning a local result value that `collect` materializes. This\ndocument records the read model, the source model, the index-realization\ndecision, the query surface, and the open items. The executable contract is\n`test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`.\n\n**Related documents:**\n- `docs/design/dao.space.index.md` — the transactor-side realization this\n  library consumes\n- `docs/design/dao.space.md` — the tuple space; *The Query Library* and\n  *Source Polymorphism*\n- `docs/design/dao.jing.md` — the storage boundary; *Physical intake versus\n  semantic composition*\n- `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md` — the distributed backend a published source\n  may be read through\n- `docs/design/adr/0001-dao-space-as-storage-boundary.md` — the storage\n  boundary decision and the monoid-homomorphism proof below\n- `docs/datomic.md` — the Datomic architecture the Peer model maps to\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe query library is an embeddable Peer over finite relations of tuples. It\nconsumes the logical tuples of each value it is handed and runs matching /\nDatalog / pull above them. An opened published index resolves its content-store\ncoordinate and manifest address below this boundary; the caller opens it and\nthe caller closes it. The query library is pure and stateless — it owns no\ndurable state, never writes, opens nothing, and enforces no schema; any schema\npolicy belongs on the write side.\n\n```\ndao.space.query/q …/match …/pull         ← the TUPLE SPACE reads\n     │  consumes finite relation values and opened indexes\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
